.\" Manual page for wifi-hotspot
.TH WIFI\-HOTSPOT 8 "2026\-10\-19" "wifi\-hotspot" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
wifi\-hotspot \- share a Wi\-Fi client uplink as an access point on the same channel
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBwifi\-hotspot\fR { up | down | restart | sync | status }
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wifi\-hotspot
runs a Wi\-Fi access point (hotspot) on a second virtual interface
.RB ( ap0 )
while the primary interface
.RB ( wlan0 )
stays associated to a Wi\-Fi network as a client, and forwards the hotspot
clients' traffic to that uplink with NAT.
.PP
It exists because of a hardware limitation of single\-radio adapters:
the hotspot must run on the
.I same channel
as the client connection.  Such adapters advertise this same\-channel
concurrency (SCC) through
.BR iw (8);
for example, the Realtek RTL8852BE
.RB ( rtw89 )
reports:
.PP
.nf
    * #{ managed } <= 1, #{ AP, P2P\-client, P2P\-GO } <= 1, total <= 2, #channels <= 1
.fi
.PP
NetworkManager cannot drive both roles itself: activating a hotspot tears
down the client connection, and its
.BR wpa_supplicant (8)
refuses to grab a second interface.  Windows instead silently puts the
hotspot on the client's channel;
.B wifi\-hotspot
does the same thing on Linux.
.PP
The command creates the
.B ap0
interface, assigns it a unique locally administered MAC address, runs
.BR hostapd (8)
pinned to the uplink's channel, and starts
.BR dnsmasq (8)
for DHCP and DNS.  It also adds the required NAT, forwarding and firewall
rules and removes them again on
.BR down .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B up
Create or ensure the hotspot on the client's current channel.  Idempotent:
if the hotspot is already running on the right channel this only refreshes
the network and firewall rules.
.TP
.B down
Tear the hotspot down: stop hostapd and dnsmasq, remove the firewall and
NAT rules and delete the
.B ap0
interface.  The client connection is left untouched.
.TP
.B restart
Equivalent to
.B down
followed by
.BR up .
Use after editing
.I /etc/wifi\-hotspot.conf
or the hotspot password.
.TP
.B sync
Re\-pin the hotspot to the client's current channel.  Does nothing if the
client is disconnected or the channel already matches.  This is what the
NetworkManager dispatcher calls on reconnect and roaming.
.TP
.B status
Show the client SSID and frequency, the AP interface, and whether hostapd,
dnsmasq and the NAT rule are up.  This is the default when no command is
given and it does
.I not
require root.
.PP
Except for
.BR status ,
the commands re\-execute themselves as root using
.BR pkexec (1)
(falling back to
.BR sudo (8)),
so they may be run by an ordinary user.
.SH OPTIONS
None.  Behaviour is controlled by the configuration file described below.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/wifi\-hotspot.conf
is a shell fragment sourced by the script.  All settings are optional and
default to the values shown.  Uncomment and edit as needed.
.TP
.B CLIENT="wlan0"
The Wi\-Fi client interface whose connection is shared.
.TP
.B AP="ap0"
The virtual access point interface created and removed by the script.
.TP
.B SSID="ArchHotspot"
The hotspot SSID.
.TP
.B COUNTRY="KE"
Two\-letter ISO 3166 country code written as
.B country_code
to the hostapd configuration.  Set it so the regulatory domain permits
your AP channel.
.TP
.B AP_ADDR="10.42.0.1"
IPv4 address assigned to the AP interface.
.TP
.B AP_PREFIX="24"
Prefix length of the hotspot subnet.
.TP
.B DHCP_RANGE="10.42.0.10,10.42.0.200,12h"
dnsmasq DHCP range (first address, last address, lease time).
.TP
.B RUN_DIR="/run"
Directory for pid and lease files.
.TP
.B MANAGE_FIREWALL=1
When 0, the script makes no firewall, forwarding or NAT changes at all.
Set this if you manage the firewall yourself (see
.BR FIREWALL ).
.PP
The Wi\-Fi password is read once from the NetworkManager profile named
after
.B SSID
(or
.BR Hotspot ),
generated if not found, and stored in
.IR /etc/hostapd/ap0.psk .
To change it, delete that file and run
.B wifi\-hotspot restart
(and reconnect the clients).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B WIFI_HOTSPOT_ELEVATE
Command used to re\-execute the script as root, e.g.
.BR sudo .
When unset,
.B pkexec
is used if available, otherwise
.BR sudo .
.SH FIREWALL
On a host whose firewall has a default\-drop policy for incoming and
forwarded traffic \- for example Arch's
.I /etc/nftables.conf
\- a plain
.BR iptables (8)
rule is
.I not
enough to make the hotspot work: a packet must be accepted by
.I every
base chain attached to a netfilter hook, so a
.B policy drop
in a native nftables chain silently overrides an
.B iptables
ACCEPT in a different chain.
.PP
When
.B MANAGE_FIREWALL
is 1,
.B wifi\-hotspot
additionally inserts a scoped
.B accept
rule for the AP interface into the existing nftables
.I ip filter
.B INPUT
and
.B forward
chains.  The rules carry the comment
.RB ` wifi\-hotspot '
and are deleted again on
.BR down .
No nftables rules are added if the
.I ip filter
table does not exist.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/wifi\-hotspot
The script.
.TP
.I /etc/wifi\-hotspot.conf
Configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/hostapd/ap0.conf
Generated hostapd configuration (mode 0600), rewritten on every
.BR up .
.TP
.I /etc/hostapd/ap0.psk
The hotspot passphrase (mode 0600).
.TP
.I /run/hostapd\-ap0.pid
hostapd pid file.
.TP
.I /run/dnsmasq\-ap0.pid
dnsmasq pid file.
.TP
.I /run/dnsmasq\-ap0.leases
dnsmasq DHCP leases.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/wifi\-hotspot.service
Oneshot unit that brings the hotspot up at boot.
.TP
.I /etc/NetworkManager/dispatcher.d/90\-wifi\-hotspot
Dispatcher hook that runs
.B sync
on connect or roaming and
.B down
on disconnect.
.SH NOTES
.TP
.B Same channel is mandatory
The AP is always pinned to the client's channel; this is a limit of the
radio, not of the tool.  The dispatcher does the pinning automatically
when you roam.
.TP
.B Throughput is roughly halved
One radio time\-shares between the two roles, on both links.  Share an
ethernet uplink instead for full speed.
.TP
.B 5 GHz DFS channels
Channels 52\-64 and 100\-144 require radar detection
.RB ( 802.11h )
and add a channel availability check (CAC) delay; non\-DFS 5 GHz and
2.4 GHz channels start immediately.
.TP
.B Regulatory domain
The AP can only use channels allowed for
.B COUNTRY
in the current regulatory domain.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure \- for example hostapd could not start, the client is not
connected, or a required program is missing.  Diagnostic messages are
printed to standard error and, when running under systemd,
.B wifi\-hotspot.service
is logged to the journal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Bring the hotspot up and check it:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# wifi\-hotspot up
$ wifi\-hotspot status
client wlan0 :  SSID: MyNet  freq: 5180.0
ap     ap0   :  type AP  channel 36 (5180 MHz)
hostapd      : running
dnsmasq      : running
nat          : ? (need root)
.RE
.fi
.PP
Re\-create the hotspot after changing the SSID or password, then tear it
down:
.PP
.nf
.RS
# wifi\-hotspot restart
# wifi\-hotspot down
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hostapd (8),
.BR dnsmasq (8),
.BR iw (8),
.BR nmcli (1),
